Z||000006||85Josh||05-04-2001||12:19 AM||||I'd appreciate it, Mach10, I really don't know Regedit very well, or what exactly to look for.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mach10||05-04-2001||12:27 PM||||Ok, here's your solution:<BR>Go to "Start" --&gt; "Run" and type in "regedit"<BR>You will get a program window up called "registry editor. From there, you will see 6 classes:<BR>HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT<BR>HKEY_CURRENT_USER<BR>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE<BR>HKEY_USERS<BR>HKEY_CURRENT_CONFIG<BR>HKEY_DYN_DATA<P>The one that you are interested in is "HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE." Click the [+] box beside it to expand the tree. <BR>You will see a list of sub-classes. Look for the one that says "Software" and expand it. It will be BIG. Scroll all the way down to a sub-class called "Microsoft" and expand that one. Then scroll down to "Windows" and expand that one too. Under windows, you will see the first sub-class labelled "CurrentVersion." Click on the sub-class NOT THE EXPAND! On the variables window (the right side) you will see a whole list of stuff pop up. Scroll down the list till you see "ProductKey" That will contain your product key.<P>There. Here's the clickpath, in case you get lost:<BR>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE -&gt; Software -&gt; Microsoft -&gt; Windows -&gt; CurrentVersion --&gt; ProductKey||||reg||6
